//! Dense linear algebra on core's `Array`: products, factorizations as
//! types (`Lu`, `Cholesky`, `Ldlt`, `Qr`, `Svd`, `Eigh`, `Eig`, `Schur`),
//! solvers and matrix functions. Every routine takes `Matrix<T>` /
//! `Vector<T>` (`Array<T, 2>` / `Array<T, 1>`, views included) for `T` in
//! f64, f32, `Complex<f64>`, `Complex<f32>` (`Scalar`), or the real two
//! (`Real`) where the algorithm is real only. Bad input throws a
//! `LinalgError` that says what to call instead; nothing panics except
//! the operators of `ops`, which cannot throw and panic on a shape
//! mismatch as core's broadcast operators do.
//!
//! Pure Nabi: no BLAS or LAPACK, so every target, wasm included, gets
//! the same results.
//!
//! ## Tutorial
//!
//! Most numerical work comes down to matrices: a system of equations, a
//! least-squares fit, the modes of a structure. `linalg` handles dense
//! matrices; [`sparse`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/sparse/latest)
//! handles the large ones that are mostly zeros. Both work on core's
//! `Matrix<f64>` and `Vector<f64>` ([Arrays and
//! numerics](https://docs.nabi-lang.org/latest/learn/21-arrays-and-numerics.html)),
//! so their results feed straight into everything else.
//!
//! ### Matrices and vectors
//!
//! A `Matrix<f64>` is a two-dimensional array; write one as nested lists
//! with the type given. With the `ops` feature (on by default), `@` is the
//! matrix product, `\` solves and `†` is the transpose (the conjugate
//! transpose for complex matrices). The dot operators (`.+`, `.*`) work
//! element by element, as in core.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Matrix, Vector};
//! use linalg::ops::*;
//!
//! let a: Matrix<f64> = [[2, 1], [1, 3]];
//! let v: Vector<f64> = [1, 2];
//! println(a @ v);
//! println(a† @ a);
//! println(a .* 10.0);
//! println(linalg::eye::<f64>(2));
//! # assert((a @ v).to_list() == [4.0, 7.0]);
//! # assert((a† @ a)[1, 1] == 10.0);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! [4.0, 7.0]
//! [[5.0, 5.0], [5.0, 10.0]]
//! [[20.0, 10.0], [10.0, 30.0]]
//! [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]]
//! ```
//!
//! `a @ v` panics when the shapes do not fit, the way `.+` does;
//! `linalg::matmul(a, b)` is the same product as a call that throws a
//! `LinalgError` instead.
//!
//! ### Solve a system
//!
//! Three unknowns, three equations: `a x = b`. `linalg::solve` factors `a`
//! (LU with partial pivoting) and solves; `a \ b` is the same as an
//! operator, returning a result to unwrap with `get`. The residual
//! `a x - b` shows how close the answer is.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Matrix, Vector};
//! use linalg::ops::*;
//!
//! let a: Matrix<f64> = [[4, 1, 0], [1, 3, 1], [0, 1, 2]];
//! let b: Vector<f64> = [1, 2, 3];
//!
//! let x = try linalg::solve(a, b);
//! println(f"x = {x:.4}");
//! println(f"residual {linalg::norm(a @ x .- b) < 1e-12}");
//!
//! let same = try (a \ b).get();
//! println(same.all_close(x));
//! # assert((x[0] - 2.0 / 9.0).abs() < 1e-12 && (x[2] - 13.0 / 9.0).abs() < 1e-12);
//! # assert(same.all_close(x));
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! x = [0.2222, 0.1111, 1.4444]
//! residual true
//! true
//! ```
//!
//! To solve with the same matrix many times, factor it once:
//! `linalg::lu(a)` (any square matrix) or `linalg::cholesky(a)` (symmetric
//! positive definite, twice as fast) return a factorization with `solve`,
//! `det` and `inv`.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Matrix, Vector};
//!
//! let a: Matrix<f64> = [[4, 1, 0], [1, 3, 1], [0, 1, 2]];
//! let c = try linalg::cholesky(a);
//! let rhs: List<Vector<f64>> = [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0]];
//! for b in rhs {
//!     println(f"{try c.solve(b):.4}");
//! }
//! println(f"det {(try linalg::lu(a)).det():.1}");
//! # assert(((try c.solve([1.0, 0.0, 0.0]))[0] - 5.0 / 18.0).abs() < 1e-12);
//! # assert(((try linalg::lu(a)).det() - 18.0).abs() < 1e-9);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! [0.2778, -0.1111, 0.0556]
//! [-0.1111, 0.4444, -0.2222]
//! det 18.0
//! ```
//!
//! ### Fit a line by least squares
//!
//! More equations than unknowns: there is no exact solution, so least
//! squares finds the one that makes the squared residuals smallest. For a
//! line `y = c0 + c1 t`, each data point is a row `[1, t]`. `linalg::lstsq`
//! solves it through a QR factorization, which keeps its accuracy where the
//! normal equations `aᵀa x = aᵀy` lose digits.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Matrix, Vector};
//!
//! let t: Vector<f64> = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4];
//! let y: Vector<f64> = [1.1, 2.9, 5.2, 7.1, 8.8];
//! let a: Matrix<f64> = Matrix::from_fn([5, 2], |i: [usize; 2]| -> f64 { if i[1] == 0 { 1.0 } else { t[i[0]] } });
//!
//! let c = try linalg::lstsq(a, y);
//! println(f"y = {c[0]:.3} + {c[1]:.3} t");
//! # assert((c[0] - 1.1).abs() < 1e-9 && (c[1] - 1.96).abs() < 1e-9);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! y = 1.100 + 1.960 t
//! ```
//!
//! `stats::ols` does the same fit and adds standard errors and p-values for
//! each coefficient
//! ([`stats`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/stats/latest)).
//!
//! ### Eigenvalues and the SVD
//!
//! `linalg::eigh` takes a symmetric matrix and returns its eigenvalues in
//! rising order with orthonormal eigenvectors as columns. Here it finds the
//! natural frequencies of three masses on springs: the eigenvalues of the
//! stiffness matrix are the squared frequencies.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Matrix;
//!
//! let k: Matrix<f64> = [[2, -1, 0], [-1, 2, -1], [0, -1, 2]];
//! let e = try linalg::eigh(k);
//! println(f"squared frequencies {e.values():.4}");
//! println(f"frequencies {e.values().map(|v: f64| -> f64 { v.sqrt() }):.4}");
//! # // 2 - sqrt(2), 2, 2 + sqrt(2)
//! # assert((e.values()[0] - 0.5857864376269049).abs() < 1e-12);
//! # assert((e.values()[2] - 3.414213562373095).abs() < 1e-12);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! squared frequencies [0.5858, 2.0000, 3.4142]
//! frequencies [0.7654, 1.4142, 1.8478]
//! ```
//!
//! The singular value decomposition works on any matrix. Its singular
//! values show the rank and how well conditioned a matrix is: the ratio of
//! the largest to the smallest is the condition number, and `linalg::cond`
//! computes it.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Matrix;
//!
//! let m: Matrix<f64> = [[1, 2], [2, 4.001]];
//! let s = try linalg::svd(m);
//! println(f"singular values {s.s():.5}");
//! println(f"condition number {(try linalg::cond(m)):.0}");
//! # assert(((try linalg::cond(m)) - 25008.0).abs() < 0.5);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! singular values [5.00080, 0.00020]
//! condition number 25008
//! ```
//!
//! A condition number of about 2.5 x 10<sup>4</sup> means a solve can lose
//! more than four of the sixteen digits a `f64` carries: the two rows are
//! almost the same equation.
//!
//! ### What next
//!
//! - [`sparse`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/sparse/latest): the
//!   same work for large matrices that are mostly zeros, with iterative and
//!   direct solvers.
//! - [`stats`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/stats/latest):
//!   distributions, tests and regression.
//! - The rest of this package: QR, Schur, `expm`, matrix functions.
//!

